WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.707
There is no single rule for handling every missing value.

00:00:03.707 --> 00:00:06.552
Sometimes dropping a row is reasonable.

00:00:06.552 --> 00:00:09.457
Sometimes filling the value is appropriate.

00:00:09.457 --> 00:00:15.147
Sometimes the missing value should remain because the missingness itself is meaningful.

00:00:15.147 --> 00:00:20.855
And sometimes the best decision is to investigate the source of the data before doing anything.

00:00:21.514 --> 00:00:26.626
In the example, we use the median to fill a missing satisfaction score.

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:29.366
This is only a teaching example.

00:00:29.366 --> 00:00:37.711
In a real project, you should first ask whether the median makes sense for that variable and for the analytical purpose.

00:00:37.711 --> 00:00:46.006
Be careful with automatic rules such as filling every numeric column with the mean or deleting every row that contains

00:00:46.006 --> 00:00:52.711
any missing value. Those choices can introduce bias or remove useful information.

00:00:52.711 --> 00:00:58.250
The important habit is to connect each cleaning decision to a reason that you can explain.
